🔹 6. try-catch Block

Definition:
Used to handle exceptions by wrapping risky code.

Syntax:
try {
    // risky code
} catch (Exception e) {
    // handling code
}

Example:
try {
    int a = 10 / 0;
} catch (ArithmeticException e) {
    System.out.println("Cannot divide by zero");
}

🔹 7. Multiple Catch Blocks

Definition:
Used to handle different types of exceptions separately.

Rule:
Always write specific exception first, then general (Exception).

Example:

try {
    int[] arr = new int[3];
    arr[5] = 10;
} catch (ArithmeticException e) {
    System.out.println("Arithmetic error");
} catch (ArrayIndexOutOfBoundsException e) {
    System.out.println("Index error");
} catch (Exception e) {
    System.out.println("General error");
}


🔹 8. finally BLOCK

Definition:
Block that always executes whether exception occurs or not.

Use:
Used for cleanup operations (closing files, DB connections, etc.)

Example:

try {
    int a = 10 / 0;
} catch (Exception e) {
    System.out.println("Error occurred");
} finally {
    System.out.println("Always runs");
}


🔹 9. throw Keyword

Definition:
Used to explicitly throw an exception.

Syntax: throw new ExceptionType("message");

Example:

int age = 15;

if (age < 18) {
    throw new ArithmeticException("Not eligible");
}


🔹 10. throws Keyword

Definition:
Used in method declaration to declare exceptions.

Purpose:
Informs the caller that the method may throw an exception.

Example:

static void check() throws Exception {
    throw new Exception("Error here");
}

Usage:

try {
    check();
} catch (Exception e) {
    System.out.println(e.getMessage());
}